Generated commit message:

refactor: route queue actions through keybinding registry

Replace direct key-string handling in QueueView with an action-based dispatch
model so that the keybinding registry is the single source of truth for all
key-to-action mappings.

Previously QueueView.Update switched on raw key strings (enter, d, x, C, K, J),
meaning any key rename in the registry would silently stop working for queue
operations. The new HandleAction method accepts a pre-resolved action constant,
and root.Update calls it after resolving the key via the registry.
QueueView.Update is now a no-op stub kept only to satisfy existing call-site
contracts.

Root model message handling is extended to cover the full lifecycle of queue
mutation messages:

  - DequeueTrackMsg removes the entry at the given index and adjusts the cursor
    when it points past the end of the list.
  - BulkSelectionToggleMsg flips the Selected flag on the target entry.
  - ClearQueueMsg resets the entry slice and cursor to zero.
  - MoveTrackUpMsg and MoveTrackDownMsg swap adjacent entries and advance the
    cursor to follow the moved track.

Repeat and shuffle toggle actions now forward the new state to the player via a
repeatShuffler interface so that mpv mode and the status bar stay in sync. Stop
also resets elapsed and total seconds so the time display clears immediately.

LibraryView.render replaces the hard-coded 50-row cap with a height-aware
calculation so the visible track list scales with the terminal, falling back to
50 rows before the first WindowSizeMsg arrives.

activeViewContext now panics on an unhandled viewID instead of silently falling
back to contextGlobal, preventing new views from bypassing view-local keybinding
shadowing without a visible failure.

Tests are updated to call HandleAction directly rather than synthesising key
messages, and a new TestHelpSectionsMatchRegistry test verifies bidirectional
consistency between the registry and helpSections to catch stale entries or
missing coverage automatically.

